THE ZERO-THEFT NUMBER IS THE ONE THAT MATTERED. Case 3b fires only where nothing
else resolved the call, so a promotion from an already-resolved project symbol
would have been a regression wearing a recall figure's clothes — WI-vihop had
exactly that shape, 24 edges bound to an interface declaration. Diffing both
ways and grouping by the OLD slot is what separates them.

892 IS THE PROMOTION COUNT; 278 IS THE RECALL FIGURE. 278 land on an actual
catalogue row (`performance.now` 212, `process.on` 23, `process.hrtime` 20,
`process.getuid` 10, `process.chdir` 5, `process.send` 4, ...). The other 614 are
correctly slotted members the catalogue does not row. Stated so the larger number
is not read as recall. Twelve on-row sites read back against source; all genuine.

REFUTATION HALF. `process` is an ordinary identifier. Three tests pin that a
named import, `const process = new Worker(...)`, and
`import * as performance from 'perf_hooks'` all still refuse the global slot —
Case 3b's existing `named_imports` / `var_types` / namespace-elif guards are what
make adding a name to the set safe, and they now cover the new names too.

A DOC LIE REMOVED. The set claimed to "mirror the module names used in
javascript.yaml". It did not, and that is how this drifted. It cannot be DERIVED
the way `_derive_js_constructor_types` derives its own, because the catalogue
records no field saying how a module is REACHED (global vs import vs
